  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Designing Anonymous Video Calls**\n\nI\'m focusing on stripping down features to prioritize anonymous, end-to-end encrypted video calls, much like Google Meet but with enhanced privacy. The goal is a clean, minimal interface. I\'m prioritizing user anonymity.\n\n\n**Refining Visual Identity**\n\nI\'m now zeroing in on the application\'s visual identity, aiming for a sleek, dark Google Meet-inspired UI with rounded geometric elements and clear typography. This includes the implementation of a slate/charcoal color scheme with a crystal clear typography, the design of camera/mic indicators, meeting code display, layout switcher, and screen share controls. I also want to make sure an E2EE connection indicator is displayed.\n\n\n**Structuring Meeting Workflow**\n\nI\'m now outlining the call flow, specifically the meeting interface. The design focuses on a clean split-screen setup: a left section for starting new meetings or joining via code/link, and a right section for a live \"Green Room\" video preview. I\'m focusing on simplicity, so the join flow is seamless. For direct links, the Green Room is instantly displayed.\n\n\n**Defining Call Interface Controls**\n\nI\'m currently designing the in-call interface, focusing on key elements. A top bar will display the meeting code, E2EE status, and call timer, while a bottom bar offers controls like mic/camera toggles, screen share, and raise hand functionality. I am also implementing quick toggles for meeting details, participants, in-call chat, and device settings, with a red \"Leave call\" button for easy exit. Sharing a screen activates a spotlight with a \"Stop presenting\" prompt.\n\n\n"
    thinking_duration {
      seconds: 5
      nanos: 102232418
    }
    stop_reason: STOP_REASON_STOP_PATTERN
  }
}
