    thinking: "**Initiating Crypto Analysis**\n\nI\'m starting a deep dive into cryptographic E2EE for the video conferencing platform. My current focus is on a `src/utils/crypto.ts` module, where I\'ll utilize the browser\'s native `crypto.subtle` API. This is key to building a truly secure platform.\n\n\n**Implementing Security Verification**\n\nI\'ve got the PBKDF2 key derivation and AES-GCM encryption/decryption functions built. My focus is now the cryptographic safety verification. I\'m implementing a Short Authentication String (SAS) with a 6-digit verification code, 4 emojis, and a SHA-256 fingerprint. This will be presented in an E2EE Security Shield Modal for user verification, mimicking best practices of secure messaging. I\'ll need to transmit the fingerprint and SAS for peer verification. Next comes secure in-call chat encryption using the same AES-GCM.\n\n\n**Establishing P2P Encryption**\n\nNow I\'m transitioning to the `useWebRTC.ts` module, setting up a secure RTCDataChannel (`e2ee_channel`) for direct, low-latency, peer-to-peer encrypted transmission of chat messages and in-call reactions. This means messages will bypass the server entirely if the DataChannel is established. I\'ve built a smooth fallback to the encrypted socket relay if the DataChannel is still connecting. Furthermore, I\'ve confirmed that messages are secure; a server should receive something like `{message: { iv: \'...\', tag: \'...\', ciphertext: \'...\' }, encrypted: true}` and be unable to read the plain text of any message!\n\n\n\n"
