  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Analyzing Device Availability**\n\nI\'m currently focused on the root causes behind \"Requested device not found\" errors, specifically in the context of `getUserMedia`. My analysis is starting with scenarios involving exact `deviceId` constraints. I\'m digging into how the error manifests when specific devices are requested, but are unavailable.\n\n\n**Investigating Constraint Failures**\n\nI\'ve been examining the error thrown when using `exact` device constraints; it seems if the specified device isn\'t present, the browser generates an `OverconstrainedError` or `NotFoundError`. Using `{ ideal: deviceId }` or a fallback approach appears much safer. Additionally, I\'m noting scenarios where no webcam or microphone is present. In these cases, calls to `getUserMedia` with both `video: true` and `audio: true` result in `NotFoundError`, and the subsequent audio-only fallback can also fail if no mic is available.\n\n\n**Exploring Fallback Mechanisms**\n\nI\'m now thinking about how to handle scenarios where no devices are present or permissions are denied. The core idea is graceful degradation. If `{ video: true, audio: true }` fails because a camera is present but no mic, the app should *not* crash and should *also* attempt a fallback like `{ audio: true }`. I\'m exploring creating synthetic or mock streams or canvases to allow the user to continue using the app (viewing others, text chatting, etc.). My progress involves handling device-less or permission-denied cases.\n\n\n"
    thinking_duration {
      seconds: 2
      nanos: 631618323
    }
    stop_reason: STOP_REASON_STOP_PATTERN
  }
}
