  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Investigating Video Fallback**\n\nI\'m currently looking into how to handle situations where a user\'s camera is unavailable. Specifically, I want to determine if we can generate a silent audio stream and a black video track as a fallback, and how we could configure WebRTC transceivers in such scenarios. I\'m exploring the behavior of `RTCPeerConnection` instantiation, especially with ICE servers, to guide my approach.\n\n\n**Analyzing Transceiver Usage**\n\nI\'ve been examining the interplay between `addTransceiver` and `addTrack`. It seems calling `addTrack` can implicitly create or re-use transceivers. I\'m focusing on how this works when no tracks are initially available. I\'ve considered that reserving the audio m-line using `addTransceiver` and replacing it with a new track later is a viable solution for the fallback scenario.\n\n\n**Verifying Transceiver Track**\n\nI\'ve confirmed that using `pc.getTransceivers()` is the correct approach to find the video transceiver, even when the associated sender\'s track is null. The `receiver.track.kind` property remains consistent (\'video\' or \'audio\'), irrespective of the sender\'s track state. It is working as I expected! This is vital for `sender.replaceTrack()` to instantly update the stream.\n\n\n"
    thinking_duration {
