  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Examining Functionality**\n\nI\'m focused on the `handleLeaveCall` function within `App.tsx`. Specifically, I\'m examining the sequence: `leaveSession()`, `webrtc.endCall()`, `updateUrlWithRoom(null)`, and `setInvitedRoomCode(null)`. What\'s the complete flow? I want to fully understand the implications of this.\n\n\n**Analyzing Stream Preservation**\n\nI\'ve been digging into how the local stream is handled in `handleLeaveCall`. It seems `webrtc.endCall()` thoughtfully preserves `localStreamRef.current`. That means the user\'s camera preview in the lobby won\'t need to re-prompt for permissions, which is fantastic! I was concerned about the user experience, but it looks good!\n\n\n**Verifying Connection Lifecycle**\n\nI\'m now investigating what happens when a user leaves a call and immediately joins another. The process starts with `onMatched`, triggering `webrtc.startCall()` and then `createPeerConnection`. It cleanly closes any existing peer connection before creating a new one. I realized I needed to check `CallView.tsx`, specifically looking at how audio/video streams are handled.\nI just noticed that if the video element is not muted, and the audio element is playing the exact same stream, we get double audio! The solution is to add `muted` to the video element.\n\n\n**Refining Audio Handling**\n\nI\'m now zeroing in on audio element behavior, particularly hidden ones. It turns out that for robust playback across browsers, especially Safari, using `hidden` (`display: none`) is problematic. Instead, I\'ve confirmed that styling with `opacity-0 pointer-events-none fixed -bottom-10 -right-10 w-1 h-1` or `sr-only` is the best practice. This avoids potential audio pauses or delays in Safari/WebKit. I\'ll need to update the code to reflect this best practice.\n\n\n**Evaluating Conditional Rendering**\n\nI\'ve been reviewing the conditional rendering in `CallView.tsx`, specifically how video and avatar elements are managed based on `peerState.videoEnabled`. I\'ve realized that when video is disabled, the `<video>` is unmounted, but the remote audio continues to play, which is expected. The muted attribute on the video prevents echo when it is re-enabled.\n\n**Investigating ICE Candidate Handling**\n\nI\'m now diving into `useWebRTC.ts` to understand how ICE candidates are handled, looking at the `iceCandidatesQueue`. I see that incoming candidates are either added immediately if the peer connection is ready or queued otherwise. The queue is then drained once the peer connection is established. This suggests a robust mechanism to handle candidates arriving before the connection is fully initialized.\n\n\n"
    thinking_duration {
      seconds: 5
      nanos: 232633170
    }
    stop_reason: STOP_REASON_STOP_PATTERN
  }
}
